fix(domain): on Effect Cluster, a call's left-out optional arguments arrive as undefined

Against alchemy `packages/domain/src` at 33c10dd (2.0.0-beta.87). Apply from the
repository root with `git apply`; the text above the first `---` line is ignored.
Independent of 01 to 03 and 07: no hunk overlaps theirs.

## Problem

On Effect Cluster, a Durable-Object-style method call is sent as one RPC,
`Call({ method, args: JSON.stringify(args) })`, and the entity calls the method
with `...JSON.parse(payload.args)` (`EffectCluster/index.ts`). `JSON.stringify`
writes an `undefined` array element as `null`, so an optional parameter the
caller left out reaches the object as `null`. On Cloudflare (structured clone)
and in memory it arrives as `undefined`, and the object code is written for
that.

It matters where a method tests `=== undefined` or has a default parameter:

- `makeNode(...).list(path, after)` (`Backfill.ts`) lists a node's children
  only when `after === undefined`, and passes `startAfter: after` to the
  storage, whose cluster `list` adds `AND key > ${startAfter}` whenever it is
  not `undefined`. With `null`, every listing of the directory is empty: no
  children, and `key > NULL` matches no row. A backfill run's root sees no
  subtrees, both passes are done at once, and the run completes with no
  instance scanned. A consumer added by a deploy, or rebuilt outside the
  in-place case, never receives the history.
- `bfStart(..., waitMillis?)` and `bfConsumerReady(..., waitMillis?)` have
  `waitMillis = 0` defaults, which `null` skips; they behave only because
  `null <= 0` is true.

Every other call site passes defined arguments, or reads its optional one with
`?.` or `??`, which treat `null` like `undefined`.

## Change

`encodeCallArgs` (`DurableObjects.ts`, beside `Stub` and `Namespace`) leaves a
call's trailing `undefined` arguments out of the JSON array, and the cluster
transport uses it. The object then gets fewer arguments, and the parameters it
did not get are `undefined`, as on Cloudflare.

## Why it is safe

- Every optional parameter of an object method is trailing (TypeScript requires
  it), so this covers each one. An explicit `undefined` before a defined
  argument would still arrive as `null`; no call site passes one.
- The wire format is unchanged: still a JSON array of the arguments, only
  shorter. An object on older code receiving the shorter array sees the same
  `undefined` parameters; a caller on older code still sends `null`, as now.
- Results are not changed: they cross as `JSON.stringify(result ?? null)`, and
  no caller tells `null` from `undefined` in a result.

## Tests

- In memory (an in-process host like the `simHost` in
  `packages/integration-tests/test/Domain/DurableObjects.test.ts`, its calls
  carrying their arguments through JSON as the cluster transport does): a
  ledger booked three times, then a deploy that adds a view keyed by a
  constant over it, then one more booking. With `JSON.stringify` the view ends
  at 1 booking (only the live one); with `encodeCallArgs`, at 4.
- On Effect Cluster with Postgres (two runners on a local swarm): restoring a
  dump of the previous code's data under code that adds such a view, the view
  holds every earlier write; before the change its backfill run logged
  `subtrees=0` and the view held nothing from before the deploy.

